WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.320
Spin
In this video, we're going to start looking

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.910
at something called Spin.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.190
Spin was something that was only discovered
when people started experimenting on very

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.960
small things like electrons.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.939
I'm going to tell you about some of the experimental
facts that they found.

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:19.140
Firstly, what is it?

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.019
We know that normal objects can have all kinds
of measureable properties like

00:00:23.019 --> 00:00:24.590
Position,
Speed,

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.840
Temperature
etc

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.630
Spin is another one of these properties that
you can measure of a particle.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.780
And in the same way that a particle can have
different positions and can change it's position,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.910
the spin of an object can take different values
and change over time.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:46.370
But unlike those other properties, it's hard
to describe what it actually is intuitively.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.720
The best I can do is say that the spin of
an particle measures how magnetic it is, and

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.420
decides which direction the magnetic field
is pointing.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.460
The problem with this statement is that it
makes particles seem like they act like tiny

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.719
little bar magnets.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:13.680
Actually they don't, because as we'll see,
spin is a very strange thing.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.479
From here on in we're going look at the spin
of electrons.

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:24.810
Whenever we measure spin, we have to pick
a direction, aka an axis, to measure it in.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:30.079
Let's call the direction pointing east the
x axis, and the direction pointing north the

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:32.080
y axis.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.330
Then say I happen to know that an electron
has spin of some strength that I'll call s,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.620
in the x direction.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.659
It's tempting to say the electron is like
a bar magnet pointing in the x direction.

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:51.490
But if that was the case, how much spin would
you expect the electron to have in the y direction?

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.899
Well the y direction is perpendicular to x,
so you normally we wouldn't expect there to

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:00.430
be any magnetic field that way at all.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:06.619
However if you measure the spin in the y direction
you will get a very weird result.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:12.870
Say we do this experiment record the results,
then do it again using a new electron and

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:13.870
keep repeating.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:20.010
Half the time you will find that the particle
not only does have spin in that direction,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.180
its strength is still s.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.720
The other half of the time?

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.810
It will still have strength s but this time
in the negative direction of y!

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.220
This is completely unexpected.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.840
But notice something.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.370
If we take the average of the results, we
get 0, which was the result we thought we'd

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.690
get.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.870
Hm, let's try another example

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.489
Let's say we have a particle with spin s in
the x direction again.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.700
We'll pick the 45 degree axis to measure spin
in.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.890
What would we normally expect?

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:59.340
Well the 45 degree angle axis is still somewhat
pointing in the x direction, so we'd expect

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.299
there to be some spin in that direction but
a smaller amount than s.

00:03:04.299 --> 00:03:09.150
We can calculate how strong we think it would
be using vectors and get that the new spin

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.680
should be s on root 2.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.549
But what happens when I measure it?

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:19.920
Again, every single time I repeat this experiment,
the electron will have spin of strength s,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.049
but this time, it will be slightly more likely
to be in the positive direction than the negative,

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:30.959
so that when I take the average of all my
results I get s on root 2.

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:38.640
Again, though each individual trial was unexpected,
the average result is what I thought I'd get.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.140
Let me summarize the two things we can learn
about the spin of an electron from these kinds

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.480
of experiments
Firstly, no matter what direction we measure

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:55.599
the spin in, its value will always be positive
s or negative s where s is equal to this number.

00:03:55.599 --> 00:04:00.560
We'll give these two possibilities some convenient
names: up and down.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:06.920
So you can for example say that an electron
is spin down in the y direction.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.120
The second weird thing is that, where ever
you'd normally expect an electron to have

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.550
a certain spin in a direction, let's say spin
equal to k, you will instead find that each

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:23.640
time you measure you only get spin up or down
but when you average the spin of each trial

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.790
you get the right result, Ie averaging your
data will give you k.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:35.300
So you see, its possible to say which direction
an electrons spin is in and its strength but

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.440
it's not really like the classical situation
with a bar magnet.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.420
With a bar magnet, knowing how strong the
magnet is, and in what direction, allows you

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.720
to find its strength in every other direction.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.830
That's not the case with spin.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:05:01.440
When there's lots of particles, on average
they act just like bar magnets, but individually

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:08.200
they act like they're equally strong in all
directions they're measured in and act randomly.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.390
We still don't know if that randomness is
true randomness though, and we still don't

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.100
know what spin really is.

